must be divisible by 9.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the sum of the known digits:\n\\[\n4 + 7 + 0 + 0 + 1 + 6 + 0 + 0 = 18\n\\]\n\nNow, we include the unknown digit \\( a \\):\n\\[\n18 + a\n\\]\n\nFor \\( 18 + a \\) to be divisible by 9, \\( a \\) must be such that \\( 18 + a \\) equals a multiple of 9. The possible values for \\( 18 + a \\) are 18, 27, 36, etc. Since \\( a \\) is a single digit (0 through 9), the only feasible value is:\n\\[\n18 + a = 18 \\implies a = 0\n\\]\nor\n\\[\n18 + a = 27 \\implies a = 9\n\\]\n\nWe need to verify which of these values makes \\( 47a001600 \\) equal to \\( 12! \\). Given the calculation of \\( 12! \\) is \\( 479001600 \\), we see that \\( a \\) must be 9.\n\nThus, the value of \\( a \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{9}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To find the digit \\( a \\) in the equation \\( 12! = 47a001600 \\), we will use the property that the sum of the digits of a number divisible by 9 must also be divisible by 9. Given that \\( 12! \\) is divisible by 9, the sum of the digits of \\( 47a001600 \\) must be divisible by 9.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the sum of the known digits:\n\\[\n4 + 7 + 0 + 0 + 1 + 6 + 0 + 0 = 18\n\\]\n\nNow, we include the unknown digit \\( a \\):\n\\[\n18 + a\n\\]\n\nFor \\( 18 + a \\) to be divisible by 9, \\( a \\) must be such that \\( 18 + a \\) equals a multiple of 9. The possible values for \\( 18 + a \\) are 18, 27, 36, etc. Since \\( a \\) is a single digit (0 through 9), the only feasible value is:\n\\[\n18 + a = 18 \\implies a = 0\n\\]\nor\n\\[\n18 + a = 27 \\implies a = 9\n\\]\n\nWe need to verify which of these values makes \\( 47a001600 \\) equal to \\( 12! \\). Given the calculation of \\( 12! \\) is \\( 479001600 \\), we see that \\( a \\) must be 9.\n\nThus, the value of \\( a \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]","id":"number_theory:51"}
